# Cursor AI Rules for Upstox PR Automation Platform

## Project Context
This is a Next.js 14+ TypeScript project that automates PR reviews and description generation using AI. It replaces N8n workflows with a maintainable web application.

## Core Technologies
- Next.js 14+ (App Router)
- TypeScript (strict mode)
- Tailwind CSS
- Groq SDK / OpenAI SDK
- Bitbucket REST API
- Slack Webhooks

## Code Style and Conventions

### TypeScript
- Always use explicit types for function parameters and return values
- Never use `any` - use `unknown` if type is truly unknown
- Prefer interfaces over types for object shapes
- Use union types for known value sets
- Import types with `import type` when importing only types

### File Organization
- API routes: `/app/api/{endpoint}/route.ts`
- Business logic: `/lib/{domain}/index.ts`
- Utilities: `/lib/utils/{utility}.ts`
- Types: `/types/index.ts` (shared types)
- Config: `/config/index.ts` (centralized configuration)

### Naming Conventions
- Files: kebab-case (`pr-review.ts`)
- Functions: camelCase (`fetchPRDetails`)
- Types/Interfaces: PascalCase (`PRDetails`, `BitbucketConfig`)
- Constants: UPPER_SNAKE_CASE (`MAX_RETRIES`)
- Components: PascalCase (`PRDashboard.tsx`)

### Function Patterns
```typescript
// ✅ Good: Explicit types, error handling, JSDoc
/**
 * Fetch PR details from Bitbucket
 */
export async function fetchPRDetails(
  config: BitbucketConfig,
  prId: string
): Promise<PRDetails> {
  try {
    // Implementation
  } catch (error) {
    console.error('Failed to fetch PR details:', error);
    throw error;
  }
}

// ❌ Bad: No types, no error handling, no docs
export async function fetchPR(config, id) {
  return fetch(url).then(r => r.json());
}
```

### API Route Pattern
```typescript
import { NextRequest, NextResponse } from 'next/server';
import type { APIResponse } from '@/types';

export async function POST(request: NextRequest) {
  try {
    const body = await request.json();
    // Process
    return NextResponse.json<APIResponse>({
      success: true,
      data: { /* result */ }
    });
  } catch (error) {
    console.error('Error:', error);
    return NextResponse.json<APIResponse>(
      {
        success: false,
        error: {
          message: error instanceof Error ? error.message : 'Unknown error',
        },
      },
      { status: 500 }
    );
  }
}
```

## Error Handling

### Always Use Try-Catch
Wrap all async operations in try-catch blocks.

### Use Retry Logic for External APIs
```typescript
import { withRetry } from '@/lib/utils/batch';

const result = await withRetry(
  async () => apiCall(),
  { maxRetries: 3, initialDelay: 1000 }
);
```

### Descriptive Error Messages
Include context in error messages:
```typescript
throw new Error(`Failed to fetch PR ${prId} from ${repo}: ${errorMessage}`);
```

## Configuration

### Never Hardcode Secrets
Always use environment variables via `/config/index.ts`:
```typescript
import { config } from '@/config';

const apiKey = config.ai.apiKey; // ✅ Good
const apiKey = 'sk-1234...'; // ❌ Bad
```

### Validate Configuration
Check config validity before using:
```typescript
import { validateConfig } from '@/config';

const { valid, errors } = validateConfig();
if (!valid) {
  throw new Error(`Invalid config: ${errors.join(', ')}`);
}
```

## Documentation

### JSDoc for Public Functions
Add JSDoc comments to all exported functions:
```typescript
/**
 * Parse a Bitbucket PR URL into components
 * @param url - Full PR URL
 * @returns Parsed components or null if invalid
 */
export function parsePRUrl(url: string): PRUrlComponents | null {
  // Implementation
}
```

### Inline Comments for Complex Logic
Add comments for non-obvious code:
```typescript
// Split diff by file boundaries (diff --git pattern)
const fileDiffs = diff.split(/^diff --git/gm);
```

### Update README.md
Update README when adding new features or changing architecture.

## Git Commits

### Conventional Commit Format
Use conventional commit messages:
- `feat:` - New feature
- `fix:` - Bug fix
- `chore:` - Maintenance task
- `docs:` - Documentation update
- `refactor:` - Code refactoring
- `test:` - Test addition/update
- `style:` - Code style change

Examples:
```
feat: implement Bitbucket API integration
fix: handle empty PR descriptions correctly
chore: update dependencies
docs: add API endpoint documentation
```

### Atomic Commits
One logical change per commit. Don't mix feature implementation with refactoring.

## Testing (Future)

When implementing tests:
- Place tests next to the code: `lib/bitbucket/index.test.ts`
- Test happy paths and error cases
- Mock external APIs
- Use descriptive test names

## Performance Considerations

### Batch Processing
Use batch utilities for processing multiple items:
```typescript
import { processBatch } from '@/lib/utils/batch';

await processBatch(items, async (item) => {
  // Process item
}, { batchSize: 10, concurrency: 3 });
```

### Rate Limiting
Be mindful of API rate limits. Add delays if necessary.

### Avoid N+1 Queries
Fetch related data in bulk when possible.

## Security

### Secrets
- Never log API keys or passwords
- Never commit secrets to git
- Use environment variables for all secrets

### Input Validation
Validate all external inputs:
```typescript
if (!prId || typeof prId !== 'string') {
  throw new Error('Invalid PR ID');
}
```

### Authentication
Use proper authentication for all API calls.

## Common Patterns

### N8n to Next.js Translation
| N8n Node | Next.js Pattern |
|----------|-----------------|
| Webhook | API Route |
| HTTP Request | fetch() in /lib |
| Code Node | TypeScript function |
| Split In Batches | processBatch() utility |
| AI Generation | SDK call in /lib/ai |

### When Adding New Features
1. Create/update types in `/types/`
2. Implement logic in `/lib/{domain}/`
3. Wire up in `/app/api/` route
4. Add config to `/config/` if needed
5. Update documentation
6. Commit with descriptive message

## Helpful Commands

```bash
# Development
npm run dev              # Start dev server
npm run build            # Build for production
npm run lint             # Run ESLint

# Git
git status               # Check status
git add .                # Stage all changes
git commit -m "feat: ..." # Commit with message
git push                 # Push changes
```

